"""
Plain-English glosses of the GDPR articles, used for MATCHING only.

What these are for
------------------
The GDPR is not written in plain English. Article 5 says "personal data
shall be processed lawfully, fairly and in a transparent manner". A
privacy policy says "we will never sell your personal information". Those
two share almost no vocabulary, so a semantic search over the raw legal
prose retrieves by topic drift: for the purpose-limitation clause it
returns Article 17 on erasure, because both mention personal data.

These glosses bridge that gap. Each one is a short plain summary of an
article, written from the article's own text, so that a plain-English
clause matches the article it actually bears on.

What these are NOT
------------------
They are not a citation, and they are not shown to a reader. The passage
the app puts on screen is always the legal text from the law index. The
gloss never leaves the retrieval layer. If a gloss were ever shown to a
reader as law, it would be a fabricated quotation, which is the one thing
this project must never do.

Each gloss is written to describe what the article REQUIRES of a
controller, because that is the thing a privacy policy clause is about.
"""

ARTICLE_GLOSS = {
    3: "What counts as personal data and what the rules apply to. Personal data is any information about an identified or identifiable person. Special categories such as health, race and political opinion get extra protection. This article sets out the general scope of the Regulation.",
    4: "The definitions. Personal data means any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person. Processing means any operation performed on personal data. Controller means the organisation that decides why and how data is processed. Processor means the organisation that processes on the controller's behalf.",
    5: "The principles of processing. Personal data must be processed lawfully, fairly and transparently. It must be collected for specified, explicit and legitimate purposes and not further processed incompatibly with them. It must be adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary. It must be accurate and kept up to date. It must be kept no longer than necessary. It must be processed securely. The controller is responsible for, and must be able to demonstrate, compliance.",
    6: "The lawful bases for processing. Processing is lawful only if it has a legal basis. The bases are consent, contract, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, and legitimate interests. For special categories of personal data there are further, stricter bases. Every processing activity must have one.",
    7: "Conditions for consent. Consent must be freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous. Where processing is based on consent, the controller must be able to demonstrate that the data subject consented. A request for consent must be clearly distinguishable, in plain language. The data subject may withdraw consent at any time, and withdrawal must be as easy as giving consent.",
    8: "The conditions for children's consent to information society services. Where consent is given for an information society service offered directly to a child, and the child is below the age at which consent can be given under national law, the consent must be given by the holder of parental responsibility. The controller must make reasonable efforts to verify that.",
    9: "Processing of special categories of personal data. Processing of data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union membership, genetic data, biometric data, health data, or data concerning sex life or sexual orientation is prohibited unless one of the specific exceptions applies. Consent is only one of those exceptions.",
    10: "Processing of personal data relating to criminal convictions and offences. This is prohibited unless it is carried out under the control of official authority, or when authorised by Union or Member State law with appropriate safeguards. It is also permitted where the processing is necessary for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims.",
    11: "Processing which does not require identification. If the purposes do not or no longer require identification of a data subject, the controller is not obliged to maintain, acquire or process additional information in order to identify the data subject.",
    12: "Transparent information and communication. The controller shall take appropriate measures to provide information in a concise, transparent, intelligible and easily accessible form, using clear and plain language. That information must be provided in a way the data subject can verify. The controller must also facilitate the exercise of the data subject's rights without undue delay and in any event within one month.",
    13: "Information to be provided when personal data are collected from the data subject. At the time the data are obtained, the controller must tell the data subject the identity and contact details of the controller, the contact details of the data protection officer, the purposes of the processing and the legal basis, the legitimate interests if the basis is legitimate interests, the recipients of the data, whether the data will be transferred outside the European Economic Area and on what basis, the retention period, and the data subject's rights including the right to complain.",
    14: "Information to be provided when personal data have not been obtained from the data subject. Where the data come from somewhere other than the data subject, the controller must provide the same information as in Article 13, plus the categories of personal data concerned, the source of the data, and where applicable that the data subject has the right to request access, rectification, erasure, restriction and objection. This applies within one month, or within two months where the data are used for direct marketing.",
    15: "Right of access by the data subject. The data subject has the right to obtain confirmation from the controller as to whether personal data concerning them are being processed, and if so to obtain access to that data and to the purposes of the processing, the categories of personal data concerned, the recipients, the retention period, the rights to rectification, erasure and restriction, the right to lodge a complaint, and the source of the data where not collected from the data subject.",
    16: "Right to rectification. The data subject has the right to obtain without undue delay the rectification of inaccurate personal data concerning them, and to have incomplete personal data completed, including by providing additional information, where the data are incomplete. The controller must communicate the rectification to each recipient to whom the data have been disclosed, where applicable.",
    17: "Right to erasure, the right to be forgotten. The data subject has the right to obtain from the controller the erasure of personal data concerning them without undue delay, and the controller must erase them where the data are no longer necessary for the purposes for which they were collected, the data subject withdraws consent and there is no other legal ground, the data subject objects and there are no overriding legitimate grounds, the data were unlawfully processed, the data must be erased to comply with a legal obligation, or the data were collected in relation to information society services. The exceptions are freedom of expression, legal obligation, public interest in public health, archiving and statistical purposes, and the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims.",
    18: "Right to restriction of processing. The data subject has the right to obtain restriction of processing where accuracy is contested, the processing is unlawful but erasure is not wanted, the data are no longer needed but must be kept for legal claims, or the objection is pending verification. A restricted controller must inform every recipient of the restriction.",
    19: "Notification of rectification or erasure to recipients. The controller shall communicate any rectification, erasure or restriction to each recipient to whom the personal data have been disclosed, where applicable. The controller must inform the data subject about those recipients if the data subject requests it.",
    20: "Right to data portability. The data subject has the right to receive the personal data concerning them in a structured, commonly used and machine-readable format and to have it transmitted to another controller without hindrance, where processing is based on consent or on contract and is carried out by automated means. The right also covers providing the data directly to another controller where technically feasible.",
    21: "Right to object. The data subject has the right to object at any time, on grounds relating to their particular situation, to processing based on public task or legitimate interests. The controller must demonstrate compelling legitimate grounds which override the interests of the data subject, or the processing is for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims. Where processing is for direct marketing, the data subject must be able to object at any time.",
    22: "Automated individual decision-making, including profiling. The data subject has the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them. Exceptions apply where the decision is necessary for a contract, authorised by law, or based on explicit consent. Suitable measures must be put in place to protect rights, and the data subject must be able to put forward a view and have human intervention.",
    23: "The restrictions on the right to object and the safeguards. Where processing is for legal claims, the right to object does not apply. For direct marketing, the controller must stop the processing immediately on objection. Where the controller establishes compelling legitimate grounds, it must inform the data subject and may then keep the data.",
    24: "The responsibility of the controller. The controller is responsible for, and must be able to demonstrate, compliance with the Regulation. It must implement appropriate technical and organisational measures to ensure and to be able to demonstrate that processing is performed in accordance with the Regulation. Those measures must be reviewed and updated.",
    25: "Data protection by design and by default. The controller must implement appropriate technical and organisational measures designed to implement data-protection principles effectively. By default, only personal data necessary for each specific purpose should be processed. The amount of personal data collected must be limited to what is necessary for the purposes for which they are processed.",
    26: "Joint controllers. Where two or more controllers jointly determine the purposes and means of processing, they must determine their respective responsibilities and make the relevant information available to the data subject. The essence of their agreement must be made available to the data subject.",
    27: "Representatives of controllers or processors not established in the Union. Where Article 3(2) applies, the controller or processor must designate in writing a representative in the Union, and the representative's contact details must be provided to the data subject. The representative must be given authority to be addressed in addition to or instead of the controller.",
    28: "Processor obligations. The processor must not engage another processor without prior specific or general written authorisation of the controller. If the processing is carried out by another processor, the same obligations must flow down to it. The processor must assist the controller with appropriate technical and organisational measures, and with responding to data subject requests. The processor must notify the controller if an instruction infringes the Regulation, and the processor must delete or return the data at the end of the engagement.",
    29: "Processing under the authority of the controller or processor. The processor and any person acting under the authority of the controller or processor must not process personal data except on instructions from the controller, unless required to do so by Union or Member State law.",
    30: "Records of processing activities. Each controller must maintain a record of processing activities under its responsibility. The record must include the purposes of the processing, a description of the categories of data subjects and data, the recipient categories including third countries, the transfers and safeguards, the envisaged erasure time limits, and a general description of the technical and organisational security measures. Each processor must keep a corresponding record for processing on behalf of the controller.",
    31: "Cooperation with the supervisory authority. The controller and the processor must cooperate with the supervisory authority on request, providing information on the processing, allowing access to premises, providing records, and correcting or erasing data that do not comply with the Regulation.",
    32: "Security of processing. The controller and the processor must implement appropriate technical and organisational measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk, including pseudonymisation and encryption of personal data, the ability to restore availability and access, a process for regularly testing and evaluating security, and regular testing of the effectiveness of measures. Where appropriate, encryption of personal data throughout the lifecycle and the ongoing confidentiality of processing should be ensured. Authorised personnel must be bound by confidentiality.",
    33: "Notification of a personal data breach to the supervisory authority. The controller must notify a personal data breach to the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware of it, unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons. The notification must be accompanied by the required information, and may be phased if not all the information is available at once.",
    34: "Communication of a personal data breach to the data subject. When the breach is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, the controller must communicate the breach to the data subject without undue delay. The communication must describe the nature of the breach, list the categories and approximate number of data subjects and records concerned, describe the likely consequences, and describe the measures taken or proposed to address the breach.",
    35: "Data protection impact assessment. Where a type of processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, the controller must, prior to the processing, carry out an assessment of the impact of the envisaged processing operations on the protection of personal data. This is required in particular for systematic and extensive profiling, large-scale processing of special categories, and large-scale systematic monitoring of a publicly accessible area.",
    36: "Prior consultation. The controller must consult the supervisory authority prior to processing where the impact assessment indicates the processing would result in a high risk in the absence of measures taken by the controller. The consultation must include the measures envisaged to address the risks, and the controller must not process the data until the advice has been taken into account.",
    37: "Designation of the data protection officer. The controller and the processor must designate a data protection officer where processing is carried out by a public authority or body, where the core activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale, or where the core activities consist of large-scale processing of special categories.",
    38: "Position of the data protection officer. The data protection officer must be involved in an advisory capacity on all issues relating to the protection of personal data, must have access to all data, must be consulted on the data protection impact assessment, and must report to the highest management level. The controller and processor must implement measures to ensure the officer's independence and to avoid conflicts of interest.",
    39: "Tasks of the data protection officer. The data protection officer must inform and advise on the application of the Regulation, monitor compliance, provide advice on data protection impact assessments, cooperate with the supervisory authority and act as its contact point, and act as contact point for data subject requests.",
    40: "Codes of conduct. Associations may prepare codes of conduct intended to contribute to the proper application of the Regulation. Where the code contains commitments which are binding and which are implemented by a controller or processor, it may be used to demonstrate compliance.",
    41: "Monitoring of approved codes of conduct. The supervisory authority must monitor and evaluate the implementation of a code of conduct which it has approved, and must inform the members of the association and the public of its assessment.",
    42: "Certification. Member States, supervisory authorities, the Board and the Commission shall encourage the establishment of data protection certification mechanisms, data seals and marks for the certification of controllers, processors and products and services. Certification shall be voluntary, and shall not relieve the controller of its responsibility.",
    43: "Certification bodies. Certification bodies with an appropriate level of expertise must issue and renew the certification after informing the supervisory authority, which may adopt and enforce the appropriate administrative rules. Certification bodies must carry out the appropriate verification.",
    44: "General principle for transfers. Any transfer of personal data which are undergoing processing, or which are intended to be processed after transfer, to a third country or international organisation must take place only if the conditions in Chapter V are complied with, including for onward transfers.",
    45: "Transfers on the basis of an adequacy decision. A transfer may take place where the Commission has decided that the third country ensures an adequate level of protection, and where the third country has made adequate protections available for the transfer. The Commission may repeal or suspend the decision if the level of protection is no longer adequate.",
    46: "Transfers subject to appropriate safeguards. A transfer may take place where the controller or processor has put in place appropriate safeguards and the data subject has been informed. The safeguards are a binding data protection agreement, public authority instrument, approved code of conduct, or certification.",
    47: "Transfers under derogations. A transfer may take place only in specific cases, such as explicit consent of the data subject, the necessity of the transfer for the performance of a contract or pre-contractual steps, or the transfer being necessary for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims. In those cases the transfer is without the need for additional safeguards.",
    48: "Transfers or disclosures not authorised by Union law. Any judgment or decision of a third-country authority requiring transfer or disclosure may only be recognised or enforceable on the basis of an international agreement in force between the third country and the Union. Without such an agreement, a transfer may take place on the basis of consent or the other derogations.",
    49: "Derogations for specific situations in the absence of adequacy decisions or appropriate safeguards. A transfer may take place only in specific cases, such as explicit consent, necessity for a contract, or the transfer being necessary for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims, where the transfer is occasional and necessary.",
    50: "The principles governing transfers. The principles in Chapter II, including lawfulness, fairness and transparency, purpose limitation, data minimisation, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity and confidentiality, continue to apply to further processing after a transfer. Further processing must not result in a lowering of the protection of the data.",
    51: "Supervisory authorities. Each supervisory authority shall carry out its tasks and powers in an independent and consistent manner. It must supervise the application of the Regulation and enforce it, and it must cooperate with other supervisory authorities.",
    52: "Independence of the supervisory authorities. The supervisory authority shall act in independence and free from any external influence, and shall not seek or receive instructions from any government. It must carry out its tasks in an effective manner and shall not defer to anyone on decisions on the performance of its tasks.",
    53: "Tasks of the supervisory authorities. The supervisory authority must handle requests from data subjects on the exercise of their rights, order the controller to comply, inform the controller of a breach, conduct audits, impose fines, and bring proceedings.",
    54: "Resources of the supervisory authorities. Each supervisory authority shall be granted the human resources and budgets necessary for the performance of its tasks. It shall have the power to request information, and to enter premises in order to carry out its tasks.",
    55: "Performance of the tasks of the supervisory authorities. The supervisory authorities shall act in accordance with the law and shall act in an independent manner. The authorities shall have the power to require the controller to provide information, and may enter premises in order to verify compliance.",
    56: "Rights of individuals to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority. Every data subject shall have the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority, in a Member State where the data subject lives or works, where the infringement is suspected, or where the data subject is served with the decision. No fees shall be charged.",
    57: "The right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority in the Member State where the controller or processor has an establishment. A complaint may be lodged with a supervisory authority in a Member State where the controller or processor has an establishment and where the processing takes place. The supervisory authority may decline the complaint where the complaint is manifestly unfounded or excessive.",
    58: "The right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority in the Member State of the data subject. A complaint may be lodged with the supervisory authority of the Member State where the data subject is located or employed, where the controller or processor is established, where the data subject's place of residence or place of work is located, or where the infringement is suspected.",
    59: "The powers of supervisory authorities in relation to processors established in another Member State. A supervisory authority may carry out the tasks and powers in the territory of its own Member State in respect of a processor with an establishment in another Member State, where the processing takes place in the context of the activities of an establishment in the territory of the supervisory authority.",
    60: "The cooperation and consistent application of the Regulation. The supervisory authorities shall cooperate and exchange information in a consistent manner, with the aim of applying the Regulation consistently. They shall conduct joint investigations where necessary.",
    61: "Cooperation and mutual assistance. The supervisory authorities shall provide information to each other on request without delay, and shall cooperate in the exercise of their tasks by enabling effective exchange of information and taking appropriate measures.",
    62: "Cooperation in the case of cross-border processing. The supervisory authorities shall cooperate in the case of cross-border processing, and the lead supervisory authority shall be the only competent supervisory authority for decisions on the actions against the controller or processor.",
    63: "Cooperation at the request of a supervisory authority. At the request of a supervisory authority, the supervisory authority concerned shall provide all information needed, and shall enforce the decisions taken.",
    64: "The right of the Board to issue an opinion. The Board may issue an opinion where a competent supervisory authority intends to adopt any decision on the basis of grounds relating to the application of the Regulation, or where there is a disagreement about the application of the Regulation. The opinion must be adopted by two thirds of the members.",
    65: "The decision of the Board in the case of a disagreement. The Board may take a decision in the case of a disagreement where the competent supervisory authorities have adopted conflicting decisions. The decision must be addressed to the supervisory authority and be formally and directly addressed to the controller or processor.",
    66: "The right of the Commission to require the supervisory authorities to cooperate. The Commission may require the supervisory authorities to cooperate in the case of a disagreement, and may adopt a decision in the case of a disagreement where the authorities have not resolved it.",
    67: "The right of the Commission to issue an opinion on the consistency of a decision. The Commission may adopt a decision where the Board has adopted a decision, and where the Commission considers that the decision is inconsistent with the Regulation.",
    68: "The right of the Commission to require the Board to conduct an investigation. The Commission may require the Board to conduct an investigation, and to examine and report on a decision adopted by a supervisory authority.",
    69: "The reporting obligations of the supervisory authorities. Each supervisory authority shall draw up an annual report on its activities, and submit it to the Board. The report shall include the number of complaints and the outcomes, and shall be published.",
    70: "The tasks of the Board. The Board shall ensure the consistent application of the Regulation, and shall adopt opinions on the application of the Regulation, on request from a supervisory authority, and adopt decisions on disputes. It shall also issue guidelines and recommendations for the consistent application of the Regulation.",
    71: "The organisation of the Board. The Board shall be composed of one representative from each supervisory authority and one from the Commission. It shall have a secretariat, which shall support the Board in the performance of its tasks.",
    72: "The secrecy of the Board. The members of the Board and the staff of the secretariat shall observe confidentiality in respect of their tasks. They shall not disclose information they have received in that capacity.",
    73: "The committees of the Board. The Board shall create working groups and committees for the handling of specific tasks. The members of the committees shall be appointed by the Board from among its members or experts.",
    74: "The chair of the Board. The Board shall elect a chair from among its members. The chair's term of office shall be two years and may be renewed once. The chair shall not be the representative of a supervisory authority which has initiated the proceedings.",
    75: "The Secretariat. The Secretariat shall support the Board in the performance of its tasks. It shall carry out the administrative and organisational tasks of the Board.",
    76: "The confidentiality of the Secretariat. The Secretariat shall observe confidentiality in respect of its tasks. It shall not disclose information it has received in that capacity.",
    77: "The right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority. Every data subject shall have the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority, in a Member State where the data subject lives or works, where the infringement is suspected, or where the data subject is served with the decision. No fees shall be charged.",
    78: "The right to an effective judicial remedy against a supervisory authority. Each natural or legal person shall have the right to an effective judicial remedy against a legally binding decision of a supervisory authority, where they consider that their rights have been infringed.",
    79: "The right to an effective judicial remedy against a controller or processor. Each natural or legal person shall have the right to an effective judicial remedy where they consider that their rights have been infringed by a processing of personal data. This right is without prejudice to any available administrative remedy.",
    80: "Representation of data subjects. Where a data subject has appointed a representative, the supervisory authority shall deal with the data subject through that representative, although the data subject may act directly. The representative's contact details must be provided to the supervisory authority.",
    81: "Suspension of proceedings. Where a complaint has been lodged, the supervisory authority may suspend the proceedings where it considers the complaint to be manifestly unfounded or excessive, or where the complainant withdraws the complaint. Suspension must be notified.",
    82: "Right to compensation and liability. Any controller or processor involved in processing is liable for the damage caused by such processing. A controller or processor shall be exonerated where it proves that it is not responsible for the damage. Where several controllers or processors are involved, they shall be jointly and severally liable.",
    83: "General conditions for imposing administrative fines. Administrative fines shall, depending on the circumstances of each individual case, be imposed where the infringements are intentional or negligent, where the processing infringes the basic principles of the Regulation including lawfulness and transparency, where the data subject's rights are infringed, or where the obligations of the controller under the Regulation are infringed.",
    84: "Penalties. Member States shall lay down the rules on other penalties applicable to infringements of the Regulation and shall take all measures necessary to ensure that they are implemented, including for infringements by public authorities.",
    85: "Processing and freedom of expression and information. This Regulation does not apply to processing for the purpose of journalistic, academic, artistic or literary expression. Personal data appearing in a work or performance is processed only if the processing is necessary for the enjoyment or the establishment of that work.",
    86: "Processing and public access to official documents. This Regulation does not apply to the processing of personal data by public authorities for the purpose of performing their tasks. The processing of personal data in official documents is subject to the national law of the Member State.",
    87: "Processing of the national identification number. Where a Member State has a national identification number, the processing of that number for the purposes of identifying the data subject is allowed in respect of a controller's activities which are required by law. The additional information such as the sex, date of birth, place of birth and personal characteristics may be processed in accordance with national law.",
    88: "Processing in the context of employment. In the context of employment, the processing of personal data shall be carried out on the basis of Union or Member State law or of a collective agreement pursuant to Member State law which provides for the protection of the rights of the data subject. The rules on the transfer of personal data to a third country or international organisation apply to such transfers.",
    89: "Safeguards and derogations relating to processing for archiving purposes, for scientific or statistical purposes and for historical research. Processing for archiving and research purposes is allowed where the purposes are served by the processing of personal data which do not relate to an identified or identifiable natural person, or where the processing is for archiving purposes in the public interest, scientific or historical research purposes or statistical purposes, subject to suitable safeguards. Where the purposes are met by non-automated processing, the data subject must have given explicit consent, or the processing is necessary for the performance of a contract, and the processing does not involve the use of the personal data for measures or decisions directed at particular data subjects.",
    90: "Obligations of secrecy. The controller and the processor and their personnel shall maintain the secrecy of the personal data and the confidentiality of the processing during the whole period of their employment, and also after the termination of that employment.",
    91: "Existing data protection rules of churches and religious bodies. This Regulation does not apply to processing by a competent church or religious body, where the processing is carried out in accordance with the law, doctrine or other rules of that church or body.",
    92: "Exercise of the delegation. The Commission may adopt delegated acts to supplement or amend the Regulation, where necessary to adjust the technical elements of the definitions, the detailed application of the rules on the legal bases, the rules on the information to be provided, and the rules on the security of processing.",
    93: "Committee procedure. The Commission shall be assisted by a committee. Where the committee procedure is applied, the Commission shall publish a draft of the delegated act and give the Committee and the Commission time to send comments.",
    94: "Repeal of Directive 95/46/EC. The Directive is repealed and references to it are construed as references to this Regulation. Member States shall adopt and publish the laws, regulations and administrative provisions necessary to comply with this Regulation before a specified date.",
    95: "Relationship to Directive 2002/58/EC. This Regulation does not affect the rules of Directive 2002/58/EC on the processing of personal data in the electronic communications sector. In relation to processing of personal data in the electronic communications sector, the provisions of this Regulation shall apply in addition to the provisions of Directive 2002/58/EC.",
    96: "Relationship to previously concluded agreements. Member States may maintain and adopt agreements with third countries and international organisations. Agreements concluded before the entry into force of this Regulation which conflict with the provisions of this Regulation continue to apply until they are amended or replaced.",
    97: "Commission reports. The Commission shall submit a report on the application of this Regulation, covering the functioning of the data protection and data security mechanisms in the Union and the functioning of the European Data Protection Board. The report shall be accompanied by proposals for amending the Regulation where appropriate.",
    98: "Review of other Union legal acts on data protection. The Commission may submit proposals for amending other Union legal acts on the protection of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data, where it considers that the relevant legal act is inconsistent with this Regulation.",
    99: "Entry into force and application. This Regulation shall enter into force on the twentieth day following that of its publication in the Official Journal. It shall apply from 25 May 2018.",
}
